\description{
Simulates an exposure, a set of mediating features spread over one or more
views, and an outcome, on top of realistic null multi-view data from a
fitted template. The design is domain-agnostic: any view and any feature
can act as a mediator.
}
\details{
The model has three parts.
\enumerate{
\item \strong{Exposure.} \code{T} is binary (\code{exposure.type = "binary"}, with
\code{P(T = 1) = exposure.prevalence}) or continuous (standard normal). Its
standardized version is \verb{T* = (T - mu_T) / sd_T}.
\item \strong{Exposure to mediators (a-paths).} For each mediator \code{j}, the latent
normal score from the template is replaced by
\verb{Z_j <- a_j T* + sqrt(1 - a_j^2) Z_j}, before it is mapped through the
feature's empirical quantile function. For a binary exposure this sum is
a two-component normal mixture, so it is passed through the mixture CDF
and back to the normal scale. Either way the latent score stays exactly
standard normal, so every mediator keeps its template distribution
(including zeros and bounds); the exposure shifts where each sample falls
in it. \code{a_j} is the mixing weight of the exposure: for a continuous
exposure it equals the latent correlation between exposure and
mediator; for a binary exposure the mixture correction lowers that
correlation somewhat (the realized effect is reported as \code{a_effect}).
\item \strong{Mediators to outcome (b-paths).} With mediators standardized by their
template mean and SD (\verb{M~_j}),
\verb{Y = DE * T + sum_j b_j M~_j + sum_k g_k M~_k1 M~_k2 + sigma * e}, where the
optional interaction terms use pairs of mediators (possibly from
different views).
}

Path sizes are set from the requested total indirect effect: \code{a_j = b_j}
in absolute value, with signs from \code{dir.a} and \code{dir.b}. The b-paths are then
rescaled so that the population natural indirect effect equals
\code{TIE * dir.a * dir.b} exactly, computed by Monte Carlo with \code{n.mc} paired
counterfactual draws per replicate. With a \code{"random"} direction (or, for
sequential mediation, negative links) the
pathway-specific effects can cancel, so their summed absolute size is set
to \code{TIE} instead and the net NIE is reported as truth. The true natural indirect effect (NIE), natural direct effect
(NDE = \code{DE}) and total effect (TE), the indirect effect carried by each view,
and each mediator's a-path, b-path and indirect contribution are returned as
truth. Effects are for the contrast \code{T = 1} versus \code{T = 0} (one SD for a
continuous exposure), in outcome units. For a continuous exposure the
mediators respond non-linearly to the exposure, so the truth also includes
\code{NIE_linear}, the indirect effect implied by each mediator's population
linear regression on the exposure (\code{a_slope}), which is what
product-of-coefficients estimators target; for a binary exposure the two
coincide.

For a binary outcome, \code{NIE}, \code{NDE} and \code{TE} (and \code{TIE}, \code{DE}) are
conditional effects on the linear-predictor (log-odds) scale. Because odds
ratios are non-collapsible, the natural (marginal counterfactual) effects
differ; they are given in \code{truth$mediation$binary} as risk differences,
odds ratios and log odds ratios.

In views that are closed compositions, renormalizing each sample after the
mediators are shifted changes the other features of the view too. The
column \code{exposure_effect} gives the true exposure effect on every feature
(standardized units, fitted scale); it is 0 for non-mediators except
through such closure.

All truth refers to the fitted (transformed) scale; see \code{back.transform}.

\strong{Sequential mediation.} With \code{stages}, the mediators are grouped into
ordered stages of views, for example \code{list("Microbiome", "Metabolome")}
for exposure -> microbes -> metabolites -> outcome. Each mediator of stage
\code{s > 1} is linked to mediators of stage \code{s - 1} (its parents): its latent
score becomes \verb{W_j = a_j T* + sum_k d_jk W_k + c_j Z_j}, where \code{W_k} are
the parents' latent scores (after their own exposure effect), \code{Z_j} is the
mediator's template latent score and \code{c_j} keeps the variance exactly 1
(allowing for the template correlation between the views), so every
mediator still keeps its template distribution. Links can be many-to-many:
one parent may feed several children and one child may have several
parents. \code{stage.links} sets the pattern, the share of each child's latent
variance explained by its parents, and the link signs; \code{stage.direct} scales
the direct exposure paths of each stage (0 for a stage reached only
through the previous one). The links are defined on the latent scale, so
in a closed composition the child depends on its parent before the
renormalization of the parent's view. For skewed or sparse parents (for
example microbiome relative abundances) the observed values are a
non-linear function of the latent score, so linear estimators on the
observed scale attribute part of the chain to the direct path.

The natural indirect effect is split into natural path-specific effects,
one for every path \verb{T -> stage r -> ... -> stage s -> Y}: for two stages,
\code{T -> M1 -> Y}, \code{T -> M1 -> M2 -> Y} and \code{T -> M2 -> Y}. They are computed
by the paired Monte Carlo with cross-world counterfactuals, switching the
paths on one at a time (later-starting paths first: \code{T -> M2 -> Y}, then
\code{T -> M1 -> M2 -> Y}, then \code{T -> M1 -> Y}), so they add up to the NIE. By
default all b-paths share one calibration scale, as without stages;
\code{stage.share} instead fixes the share of the indirect effect carried by
the mediators of each stage (exact without interactions). For example,
\code{stage.direct = c(1, 0)} with \code{stage.share = c(0, 1)} gives a pure chain
\code{T -> M1 -> M2 -> Y}, and \code{stage.links = list(strength = 0)} gives two
parallel mediator sets whose only association is the template crosstalk,
a null for the link between them.

\code{effect.type} gives null scenarios for type I error studies: \code{"null_a"}
(mediators affect the outcome but not the exposure), \code{"null_b"} (the exposure
affects the mediators but they do not affect the outcome) and \code{"null_ab"}
(neither). The true NIE is 0 in all three. In \code{"null_a"} the b-paths are
calibrated as in the corresponding spiked scenario, so type I error and
power runs are matched on effect sizes.
}
